📌 What is Clustering?
Clustering is an unsupervised machine learning technique used to group similar data points together based on certain features. The idea is to form clusters where:

Data points within the same cluster are very similar.

Data points in different clusters are very different.

There is no label or output variable. The algorithm must learn the structure only from the input data.

🔍 Why Use Clustering?
To find natural groupings in your data.

For data compression, pattern recognition, customer segmentation, image compression, etc.

Common in exploratory data analysis (EDA).

🧠 Main Types of Clustering Algorithms
Let’s dive deep into these three:

1. Hierarchical Clustering
2. Medoid-Based Clustering (includes K-Medoids)
3. Density-Based Clustering
1️⃣ HIERARCHICAL CLUSTERING
Hierarchical clustering builds a tree of clusters, called a dendrogram. It can be:

🔼 Agglomerative (bottom-up) — most common

🔽 Divisive (top-down)

🔼 Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering (AHC)
Steps:

Start with each data point as its own cluster.

Compute pairwise distances between all clusters.

Merge the two closest clusters.

Repeat until only one cluster remains.

Visualize the merges using a dendrogram.

🔧 Linkage Methods
To decide which clusters to merge, we compute distances between clusters using one of the following:

Linkage Type	Description
Single	Distance between the closest two points (min distance).
Complete	Distance between the farthest two points (max distance).
Average	Average distance between all pairs across clusters.
Ward’s	Minimize the increase in variance after merging (used with Euclidean distance).

✅ Pros
No need to choose k (can be picked by cutting dendrogram).

Visual and intuitive.

Can reveal nested clusters.

❌ Cons
Not scalable to large datasets (O(n²) complexity).

Merging decisions are permanent (no backtracking).

Sensitive to noise and outliers.

2️⃣ MEDOID-BASED CLUSTERING (K-MEDOIDS)
🧩 What’s a Medoid?
A medoid is a real data point in a cluster whose average distance to all other points in that cluster is minimum.

🔁 K-Medoids Algorithm (Also called PAM: Partitioning Around Medoids)
Very similar to K-Means, but:

K-Means uses centroids (mean of points) which may not be actual data points.

K-Medoids uses medoids, which are always real objects.

🧠 Algorithm Steps:
Initialization:

Randomly choose k actual data points as initial medoids.

Assignment Step:

Assign each data point to the closest medoid (using any distance metric).

Update Step:

For each cluster, find the point that minimizes total intra-cluster distance (becomes new medoid).

Repeat assignment and update until medoids don’t change.

📈 Objective:
Minimize the total sum of distances between points and their respective medoids.

✅ Pros
More robust to noise and outliers than K-Means.

Medoids are interpretable (real data points).

❌ Cons
More computationally expensive than K-Means.

Less popular due to this extra cost.

✅ Use K-Medoids when:
Your dataset has noise/outliers.

You want actual representatives (medoids) for each cluster.

You use non-Euclidean distance metrics (e.g., Manhattan, Cosine, etc.).

3️⃣ DENSITY-BASED CLUSTERING (DBSCAN)
🌍 Key Idea:
Find regions of high density (clusters) separated by low-density areas (noise/outliers).

🔁 DBSCAN Algorithm:
Core point: A point with at least minPts neighbors within a radius ε.

Border point: Has fewer than minPts within ε, but is close to a core point.

Noise point: Not a core or border.

🚦Steps:
Start from an unvisited point.

If it’s a core point, grow a cluster by visiting all density-reachable points.

If it’s noise, move on.

Repeat until all points are visited.

📌 Parameters:
ε (epsilon): Radius of neighborhood.

minPts: Minimum points required to form a dense region.

✅ Pros:
Detects arbitrary-shaped clusters.

Automatically detects noise/outliers.

Doesn’t need number of clusters (k) as input.

❌ Cons:
Choosing the right ε and minPts is tricky.

Doesn’t perform well in varying density regions.

Sensitive to parameter tuning.

🧪 K-MEDOIDS VS K-MEANS
Feature	K-Medoids	K-Means
Cluster center	Medoid (real point)	Centroid (mean)
Sensitive to outliers	❌ No	✅ Yes
Distance metric	Any (Euclidean, etc.)	Usually Euclidean
Interpretability	✅ Medoids are real	❌ Centroids are not real points
Speed	Slower	Faster

📚 Summary
Method	Based on	Key Strengths	Key Limitations
Hierarchical	Distance + Tree	No need to choose k, dendrogram	Slow, not scalable
K-Medoids	Medoid-based	Robust to outliers, real centers	Slower than K-Means
DBSCAN	Density-based	Finds arbitrary clusters, detects noise	Sensitive to parameters









📌 1. What is a Distance Matrix?
A distance matrix is a square matrix that shows the pairwise distances between points in a dataset.

If you have n samples, the distance matrix will be of shape (n × n), and the entry at position [i, j] tells you the distance between point i and point j.

🧠 Functions in scipy for Distance Matrix
🔹 scipy.spatial.distance.pdist(X)
X: A 2D array of shape (n_samples, n_features)

Computes all pairwise distances between rows of X

Returns a condensed 1D vector of size n(n−1)/2

