📏 Distance Metrics (for pdist)
You can choose from many distance metrics when using pdist. These define how "far" two data points are from each other.

Common Metrics:
Metric	Description
'euclidean'	Straight-line (L2 norm): √(Σ(xi - yi)²)
'manhattan' or 'cityblock'	Sum of absolute differences (L1 norm): Σ
'cosine'	1 - Cosine similarity between vectors
'chebyshev'	Maximum absolute difference across dimensions
'minkowski'	Generalized distance: (Σ
'hamming'	Proportion of bits that differ
'jaccard'	(A ∪ B - A ∩ B) / (A ∪ B), good for binary data
'correlation'	1 - Pearson correlation coefficient

Example:
python
Copy
Edit
D = pdist(X, metric='manhattan')  # Or metric='cosine', 'chebyshev', etc.
Use the metric that suits your data type and clustering goal.

🧮 Linkage: What Is It?
Linkage defines how two clusters are merged in hierarchical clustering. It uses the distance matrix to compute a new inter-cluster distance.

scipy.cluster.hierarchy.linkage(D, method='...')
Where D is the output from pdist.

🔗 Linkage Methods Explained in Detail:
Linkage Type	How It Computes Inter-cluster Distance	Characteristics
Single	Minimum distance between any two points in the two clusters. min(d(x, y))	Forms elongated, "chain-like" clusters. Sensitive to noise.
Complete	Maximum distance between any two points. max(d(x, y))	Tends to form compact, spherical clusters.
Average	Mean of all pairwise distances between cluster points. avg(d(x, y))	Balanced method, good general-purpose choice.
Ward	Minimizes the increase in total within-cluster variance after merging	Results in small, equally-sized clusters. Needs Euclidean distance.
Centroid	Distance between the centroids of the clusters	Can sometimes produce inversions in dendrogram.
Median	Like centroid but uses the median instead of mean	Less sensitive to outliers than centroid.

🔍 Visual Example (Intuition):
Imagine two clusters A and B:

A = {a1, a2}

B = {b1, b2}

